1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,000
An AI planner can be right about a world that no longer exists.
In a hospital, that world changes fast.

2
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:25,000
This is GUARD, running on QNX 8.0 in the quick-start target image under QEMU.
Each part is its own process, with its own priority.

3
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:45,000
The planner only proposes motion. The supervisor checks each proposal
against its own view of the world. If every check passes, it grants a short lease.
A separate gate holds that lease and stops the robot when it runs out.

4
00:00:45,000 --> 00:01:10,000
Here the MRI zone switches on. The planner still cites the old map,
so GUARD rejects it and the robot waits.
A heartbeat-only baseline drives straight into the zone.

5
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:30,000
Now we kill the supervisor. Nothing renews the lease,
so the gate's own kernel timer stops the robot and latches.

6
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:45,000
A paused supervisor wakes up after it was replaced.
Its lease carries an old epoch, and the gate refuses it.

7
00:01:45,000 --> 00:02:00,000
A busy process starves the checks. Leases lapse, so the robot stops.
Starving GUARD can only stop motion, never extend it.

8
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:15,000
Near a crossing, a fast proposal fails the stopping-distance check.
A slower one is authorized.

9
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:30,000
Results from our host (Linux) harness:
no supervisor, a heartbeat, and GUARD.

10
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:45,000
QNX gives us message passing, timer pulses and resource managers.
GUARD is a simulation-only research prototype, not for real robots or patients.

11
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:50,000
Stale decisions can't move the robot. Find GUARD on GitHub.
